WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Claude Opus 5.5 turned 31 real U.S. government data sets into a playable civilization-style map with 25 kilometer hexes, 19 lenses, and 524 Metro views. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 246. Invidia's parse kit and data sets chart overlays come next, then things to try on the horizon, and the engineering tear down. Andre Carpathi, posting on X, drove the Claude Code desktop app on medium reasoning, with ultra-code mode on

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.340
on, and had it ingest census files plus the Bureau of Economic Analysis, or BEA, the Bureau

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:43.220
of Labor Statistics, or BLS, the Energy Information Administration, or EIA, the National Oceanic

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:48.940
and Atmospheric Administration, or NOAA, and the United States Geological Survey, or USGS,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.640
the Department of Defense, or DOD, the National Park Service, or NPS, the Federal Aviation

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:01.440
Administration, or FAA, the Federal Emergency Management Agency, or FEMA, and the Centers

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.740
for Disease Control and Prevention, or CDC, went into, so, elevation, land cover, gross

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:15.840
domestic product, or GDP, jobs, power plants, mines, ports, airports, bases, parks, universities,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:22.480
freight flows, and disasters all sit on the same hexes. GDP maps to gold yield, San Jose

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:28.060
got a campus district, Detroit became motor city, and specializations follow real jobs,

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.440
with one in four San Jose workers being engineers, coders, or scientists.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.480
Income, degrees, faith, and disasters, each get a lens, nine advisors sit alongside.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:45.680
A city view opens every metro, and an explorer turns any column across more than 3,100 counties

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.560
into tables and scatterplots tied to the hexes.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:55.140
The job ran seven hours and 45 minutes, wrote 45,000 lines of code, and the model cut

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.460
a trailer video, though Carpathi said Claude skipped some states and territories.

00:02:00.481 --> 00:02:05.361
The demo is live to browse and extend, with no fine-tuning behind the reconciliation.

00:02:05.901 --> 00:02:11.041
A question mark icon opens the source and the reconciliation notes, and National, Metro

00:02:11.041 --> 00:02:13.741
and County views switch with no software to install.

00:02:13.741 --> 00:02:20.581
Opus 5.6 is what Simon Willison, posting on X, is using for daily work until the allowance

00:02:20.581 --> 00:02:23.221
runs out and he drops to Opus 5.5.

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:28.361
Fable sometimes steps in as a sub-agent on those fallback sessions during long coding

00:02:28.361 --> 00:02:29.581
or research stretches.

00:02:30.538 --> 00:02:35.218
In Vidia, according to the hugging phase blog, released synthetic training sets, a fine-tuning

00:02:35.218 --> 00:02:41.038
recipe, and a D-flash 2 speculator for faster inference on Nemotron 3.5 parts.

00:02:41.038 --> 00:02:46.538
The compact document model that turns page images into structured data for retrieval augmented

00:02:46.538 --> 00:02:49.138
generation, or RAG, and agents.

00:02:49.698 --> 00:02:56.158
Three data sets are open, synthetic charts V1 with 9.98 million examples, synthetic tables

00:02:56.158 --> 00:03:03.458
V1 with 1 million, and OCR synthetic multilingual V2 with 7.9 million, all aimed at chart and

00:03:03.458 --> 00:03:04.318
table extraction.

00:03:04.978 --> 00:03:10.338
Training ran supervised fine-tuning on document understanding first, then reinforcement learning

00:03:10.338 --> 00:03:13.238
for accuracy, consistency, and reliable completion.

00:03:14.018 --> 00:03:19.618
Parse reads a page in reading order, labels headings, paragraphs, tables and charts, and returns

00:03:19.618 --> 00:03:25.098
bounding boxes, and the latest cut handles harder charts and tables, including unlabeled

00:03:25.098 --> 00:03:28.238
bar charts, reading values off axes and bar heights.

00:03:28.978 --> 00:03:33.718
Tables keep row and column layout so a shift cannot rewrite the meaning, and the synthetic

00:03:33.718 --> 00:03:38.458
pipeline renders chart and table variance with known ground truth, so the model learns

00:03:38.458 --> 00:03:43.811
to reverse that rendering. Custom lines, shaded areas, and labels can now sit on

00:03:43.811 --> 00:03:49.651
data set charts. Simon Willison wrote on his blog, placed by hand or generated by the data set

00:03:49.651 --> 00:03:55.751
agent. Those overlays mix on charts drawn from SQLite data, and a new gallery gathers every

00:03:55.751 --> 00:04:00.131
supported chart type and annotation as the library's reference for styles and variants.

00:04:00.731 --> 00:04:06.131
In a follow-up, Carpathy said the map ran on his regular clawed subscription, rather than any

00:04:06.131 --> 00:04:12.021
special work allocation. Install the latest data set charts release and try the new annotations

00:04:12.021 --> 00:04:17.041
on your own data sets, including letting the agent label a chart for you. Download the

00:04:17.041 --> 00:04:23.121
open synthetic charts V1 and synthetic tables V1 sets from hugging face and fine tune a document

00:04:23.121 --> 00:04:29.301
parser for your own rag pipelines. In the live map demo, open explorer, pick a county column,

00:04:29.581 --> 00:04:35.597
and generate a scatter plot or table that links back to the hex grid. Carpathi's public data workflow

00:04:35.597 --> 00:04:39.597
is expected to be copied by more teams, using current cloud limits.

00:04:40.217 --> 00:04:44.897
Other labs may follow Nvidia's parse recipes with more open document parsing models.

00:04:45.477 --> 00:04:50.297
Thousands of generative calls per memory build drop-away when you pop the hood on MGMM,

00:04:50.697 --> 00:04:55.877
which an archive paper presents as a swap from generative large language model, or LLM,

00:04:56.217 --> 00:04:59.937
summarization to a deterministic discriminative parser pipeline.

00:05:00.597 --> 00:05:04.857
Recurring mentions across sessions become an incidence graph that ties the same entities

00:05:04.857 --> 00:05:10.097
together. A subject-conditioned three-stage resolver decides which pass mentions a question

00:05:10.097 --> 00:05:14.857
is talking about, and the system hands back the original raw dialogue episodes instead

00:05:14.857 --> 00:05:20.637
of synthesized text. That cut also removes non-determinism and provenance loss, because

00:05:20.637 --> 00:05:26.397
later queries fetch exact source turns rather than paraphrase summaries, and no LLM rewrites

00:05:26.397 --> 00:05:31.857
history at index time. On English multi-session dialogue question answering, it beats match

00:05:31.857 --> 00:05:37.737
protocol baselines on Locomo with zero generative indexing, stays competitive on the full long

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:43.497
MemEVAL S benchmark, and leads or ties on single-session retrieval tasks where local context

00:05:43.497 --> 00:05:48.837
dominates. A downstream reader sees the precise original utterances and can verify context

00:05:48.837 --> 00:05:53.917
directly, which matters when an agent must cite exact past interactions or when repeated

00:05:53.917 --> 00:05:59.737
generative calls would blow a budget. MG Mem is a drop-in deterministic alternative when a workflow

00:05:59.737 --> 00:06:03.917
needs auditability or indexing that avoids stochastic LLM calls.

00:06:04.457 --> 00:06:09.077
Generative summarization stays the better fit only when heavy abstraction across thousands

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:12.217
of turns is the goal, and exact wording is secondary.

00:06:13.263 --> 00:06:18.123
Before we go, Simon Willison is still landing data set charts features on a weekly cadence,

00:06:18.583 --> 00:06:21.603
so another annotation pass could show up on his site this week.

